maximalNonstableSubquivers -- return the maximal subquivers that are semistable
*******************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        maximalNonstableSubquivers Q
  * Inputs:
      * Q, a *note toric quiver: ToricQuiver,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * ReturnSingletons => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value false, Whether or not to return singleton vertices
        subquivers
      * Format => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value "list",
        format for representing the subquivers
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, consisting of two
        keys: Nonsingletons and Singletons

Description
===========

This method takes all of the possible subquivers of a given quiver Q and
returns those that are not stable, and which are maximal with respect to the
weight on the quiver Q.

Subquivers are represented by lists of arrows, except in the case of subquivers
that consist of singleton vertices.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : maximalNonstableSubquivers bipartiteQuiver (2, 3)                       |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = HashTable{NonSingletons => {{0, 1, 2, 3}, {0, 1, 2, 4}, {0, 1, 2, 5},   |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : HashTable                                                               |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{0, 3, 4, 5}, {1, 3, 4, 5}, {2, 3, 4, 5}}}                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

